messages:
jonathan: Hey team — running into an odd edge-only streaming failure. Local Node (v18) streaming works fine, but on Vercel Edge the ReadableStream returns the first chunk then immediately closes. No useful error in client, server logs show 200 then a sudden connection close. Repro steps below.

jonathan: Repro (minimal):
```ts
// next.js edge route (runtime: 'edge')
import Redwood from '@redwood/inference';
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.RWD_KEY });
export const GET = async (req) => {
  // A/B redirect in middleware sometimes sets a header here
  const stream = await client.chat.stream({ model: 'rwd-13b', messages: [{ role: 'user', content: 'write a short poem' }] });
  const reader = stream.body.getReader();
  let out = '';
  while (true) {
    const { done, value } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    out += new TextDecoder().decode(value);
    console.log('chunk', value.length);
  }
  return new Response(out);
};
```

jonathan: Environment: Next 13.4 (edge), @redwood/inference 1.6.0, Vercel Edge runtime, reproduces on feature branch once every ~3 deploys. Locally (node) works every time.

support-ian: Thanks — can you paste the response headers you see in the edge logs and any proxy logs? Also does the client observe a non-200 http status or an abrupt network error?

jonathan: Headers from Vercel edge logs (redacted):
```
content-type: text/event-stream
cache-control: no-cache
x-edge-runtime: vercel-edge
transfer-encoding: chunked
date: Tue, 02 Jan 2026 12:34:56 GMT
```
The client sees first chunk then the ReadableStream returns done=true; no thrown exception. Server-side app logs show normal generation until the last chunk then nothing.

alex: :eyes: Edge-only + intermittent — two likely suspects: 1) fetch polyfill / runtime cancels when middleware sets certain response headers (redirect flow you mentioned), 2) SDK's streaming wrapper might be using getReader() + cancel()/releaseLock() patterns that interact poorly with the edge fetch implementation. Any chance your middleware issues a Response.redirect or sets a 3xx earlier in the chain?

jonathan: Yes — we do Response.redirect in middleware for a small percent of users (A/B). But it's a different request path; still, sometimes the middleware runs earlier and sets a header before the handler. We tried removing redirect and frequency dropped but didn't go away.

devex-maria: Can you try two quick things so we can narrow down: 1) reproduce without any middleware; 2) change the SDK call to use streaming via streamToWeb + Response.body.pipeTo (we added a helper in the latest patch). Also which exact SDK version? 1.6.0 you said — can you try 1.6.3?

jonathan: Tried 1.6.3 earlier today (we thought it had the fix) — still intermittently reproduces. Will try without middleware and report.

support-ian: If it helps, attach a minimal repo link; we can try deploy a repro to Vercel. If you can include a tiny toggled flag that flips the middleware redirect we can attempt to reproduce deterministically.

jonathan: Uploaded minimal repro here: https://github.com/rwd/repro-edge-stream-earlyclose (private). Steps in README. Tested without middleware for 4 deploys and still saw 1 failure.

samir: Quick theory — some edge runtimes will close the underlying network stream if response headers are mutated after body stream starts, or if Response.redirect triggers internal signal aborts. Also older fetch polyfills will call reader.cancel() when they detect early EOF. SDK previously used reader.cancel() in a finally block — that could force close on some runtimes.

samir: Can you grep for any use of Response.redirect in your edge middleware or for any custom 'location' headers being added? Also check whether you set content-length anywhere (should be chunked).

jonathan: No content-length override. We only set a x-experiment header in some cases. No explicit Response.redirect in the failing route (only in middleware). I can toggle middleware off and test more.

alex: For triage: we'll try reproducing on Vercel using your repro. If we confirm it's SDK behavior, we'll fast-track a runtime patch. In the meantime try this workaround in your handler (wrap body via transform stream rather than manual getReader loop):
```ts
// use streamToWeb helper (pseudo)
const stream = await client.chat.stream(...);
return new Response(streamToWeb(stream.body));
```
This avoids intermediate reader.cancel paths.

jonathan: Tried transform-based approach — seemed to reduce frequency but didn't eliminate. Adding debug logging to when reader.cancel() is called shows it only happens in SDK cleanup.

dev-bot: Incident created: INC-2026-451 (edge-stream-earlyclose) — assigned to eng-runtime.

alex: Assigning to eng-runtime owners @samir, @alex. Could be edge runtime fetch behavior + SDK cleanup path. We'll add additional guards to avoid calling cancel() if done=true and skip abort signals for certain runtimes.

jonathan: Thanks — I'll keep testing with the lightweight workaround. If you want, we can give temporary access to the private repro.

support-ian: Please do — that will speed up repro. Also include Vercel deployment URL and the exact deploy where you saw the failure.

jonathan: Shared access. Latest failing deploy: https://rwd-staging.vercel.app (deploy id dpl_4821). Logs attached in the repo.

samir: Noted. Next steps: 1) eng-runtime to reproduce + confirm cancel() interaction; 2) devex to add a note to streaming docs about edge caveats and recommended helper usage; 3) support to follow up with customer and collect extra logs. I'll update INC-2026-451 with reproducer and initial findings.

jonathan: Thanks everyone. Appreciate the quick triage.

support-ian: Marking as follow-up required. :thumbsup:
